title: Mass writes in the store
tags: Storage
kind: rationale
audience: dev
summary: A write to many records files one event per operation in the field's list, and one entry per part of the ids in the parts' lists, instead of a write per record; reads replay the pending events and apply the entries, a fold writes them in. The log of an add or a remove is one entry too ([[Mass operations in the log]]).

Setting a tag on ten thousand files (`mf … field set|add|remove` on a query,
[[Query endpoints]]) is one write to many records. Indexing it record by record has
two costs. Filing each record under the value it gains is cheap — the holders of one
value are neighbours in the store. Taking each record ''out'' of the value it loses is
not: that value has to be known, so every record is read first.

!! One event per operation

A field has a list of ''pending events'' (table `events`, keyed by field and a
number). A write to many records appends one, naming the operation and the records
— a bitmap of dense ids — and files nothing per record:

|!Event |!A value's holders become |
|add `v` to `B` |`∪ B`, for `v` |
|remove `v` from `B` |`∖ B`, for `v` |
|replace by `vals` on `B` |`∖ B`, then `∪ B` for the values of `vals` |

A replacement never names the values it removes. The holders of a value are what is
''stored'' — its run of the value partition or its posting, and for a `ref` its
referrers ([[Store tables]]) — with the field's events replayed over it, in order.

What cannot wait is kept exact at once, by set operations whatever the number of
records: the count of values of each record (an increment on a set of ids is a carry
from bit to bit, [[Store tables]]), the `absent` and long-text sets, the trigrams
([[Text search in the store]]: a new text's entries by value, or the ids pending by
metarecord), and the histograms for a value added or removed.

!! What reads do

* ''Equality, referrers, a text search by value'': the value's stored holders, replayed.
* ''A range, a `!=`, a scan'': the values an event names are left out of the scan of what is stored and read one by one, replayed; every other value has only lost the records a replacement was written on, which are subtracted once.
* ''A sort by walking the values'' ([[Sorting and postings]]): the values an event names are stepped over where they are stored and handed over at their place in the order, their holders replayed — a value no record held before the event included; the other values come as stored, less the replaced records. A page still costs the page (`tests/kv_events.rs`: 11 067 keys for a page of 50 over 3 000 records when such a page was fetched from the rows, under 1 000 walked). Only a named value whose key does not hold it whole — a text over 256 bytes, written to one record while events were pending — has no place in the walk: the page is then fetched from the rows.

A field without pending events — nearly all of them, nearly always — is read as
before, for one read of its (empty) list.

!! Order

While a field has events pending, a write to ''one'' record of it is an event too, of
one id: written into what is stored, it would be overwritten by the replay of an older
event. The counts and the sets above are kept at each write, in order, and never
replayed.

!! The fold

`KvStore::fold` — and a reindex, and a write that leaves a field more than 64 events
(`EVENTS_MAX`, to be measured) — writes the events into what is stored, oldest first,
and empties the list. A replacement is where its removed values are found: the
field's stored values are walked for the records it was written on, and the
histograms give back the rows they held. Until then the histogram of a field with
events counts those rows, so `mf repo check` does not hold it to the rows; the
partition and the referrers are held to them as replayed
([[Checking and reindexing a repository]]).

!! The rows: one entry per part

The events say, for a value, which records hold it. The ''rows'' of a record say
the other way round, which values it holds, and they are not written either: a
write to many records appends, to the list of every ''part'' of the ids it touches —
64 k ids, the high bits of the dense id — one entry (table `mass`, keyed by part and
operation number): the field, the kind (add, remove, replace), the values, and the
ids of that part. A mass write thus writes as many keys as it has parts, and no
record.

A record is read as what it holds, with the entries of its part that name it applied
oldest first: an add puts the value in, a remove takes it out, a replace takes the
field's rows out and puts its values in. Reading a record costs the entries of its
part, which is why a part is folded when its list passes 32 entries (`PART_MAX`, to
be measured). When no part has any entry, nothing is looked for.

The most recent write wins, a write to one record included, so a record that an entry
names carries the number of the operation that wrote each of its rows, what was taken
off it by hand (`tomb`: this value, after that number) and the fields it was emptied
of (`cleared`). Such a write loads the record with the entries applied, changes it,
and writes the result: the entries stay in the list and apply to it again, harmlessly,
since what it holds then is newer than they are. A record no entry names is written
as ever, without a number. A record's value is its rows, then — when it has any —
those numbers and marks, so one written before reads unchanged
([[Persisted format changes]]).

The fold (`KvStore::fold`, a reindex, a part past its limit) writes the entries into
the records of their part and empties the list; a metarecord that is gone is not
written back.

The log of an add or a remove is one entry as well ([[Mass operations in the log]]);
a replacement still reads every record it changes, to log what it was, and writes one
operation per record.

Positions (`tree_ref`, `hierarchy_ref`) are never part of an event: each is written
on its own ([[One position per forest]]). Nor is a `Nothing` or a text over 512 bytes
added or removed.

`tests/kv_events.rs` holds every reader to the oracle with events pending, after a
fold and after a reindex.
